Example \(\PageIndex{1}\): Determining Whether an Ordered Pair Is a Solution to a System

Determine whether the ordered pair is a solution to the system:

\[\left\{\begin{aligned}

x+4 y & \geq 10 \\

3 x-2 y & <12

\end{aligned}\right. \nonumber \]

1. \((-2,4)\)

2. \((3,1)\)\(\)

Answer

1. Is the ordered pair \((-2,4)\) a solution?

We substitute \(\boldsymbol{x}=-\mathbf{2}\) and \(\boldsymbol{y}=\mathbf{4}\) into both inequalities.

                \begin{aligned}

                x+4 y & \geq 10 \\

                -\mathbf{2}+4(4) & \stackrel{?}{\geq} 10 \\

                14 & \geq 10 \text { true }

                \end{aligned}	
                \begin{aligned}

                3 \boldsymbol{x}-2 \boldsymbol{y} & <12 \\

                3(-\mathbf{2})-2(4) & \stackrel{?}{<} 12 \\

                -14 & <12 \text { true }

                \end{aligned}	

The ordered pair \((-2,4)\) made both inequalities true. Therefore \((-2,4)\) is a solution to this system.

2. Is the ordered pair \((3,1)\) a solution?

We substitute \(\boldsymbol{x}=\mathbf{3}\) and \(\boldsymbol{y}=\mathbf{1}\) into both inequalities.

                \begin{aligned}

                \boldsymbol{x}+4 \boldsymbol{y} & \geq 10 \\

                \mathbf{3}+4(\mathbf{1}) & \stackrel{?}{\geq} 10 \\

                7 & \geq 10 \text { false }

                \end{aligned}	
                \begin{aligned}

                3 x-2 y & <12 \\

                3(\mathbf{3})-2(\mathbf{1}) & \stackrel{?}{<} 12 \\

                7 & <12 \text { true }

                \end{aligned}	

The ordered pair \((3,1)\) made one inequality true, but the other one false. Therefore \((3,1)\) is not a solution to this system.

